<P CLASS="ivy">❦</P>


	<P CLASS="justify">
	<A CLASS="note" ID="note96" HREF="
E/Roman/Texts/Theophrastus/De_signis*.html#ref96
"onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,BackRef,WIDTH,175)"
onMouseOut="nd();">96</A>
A comparison of war to stormy weather. Quoted also by
	<A HREF="
E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/De_superstitione*.html#T169b
	"TARGET="Plutarch_E"
onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,Plutarch,WIDTH,PlutarchWidth)"
onMouseOut="nd();">
Plut. <I>de Superstitione</I>, 72</A>, and by Heraclides, <I>Allegoriae Homericae</I>, 4. In both citations the Greek is corrupt.


<P CLASS="ivy">❦</P>


	<P CLASS="justify">
	<A CLASS="note" ID="note97" HREF="
E/Roman/Texts/Theophrastus/De_signis*.html#ref97
"onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,BackRef,WIDTH,175)"
onMouseOut="nd();">97</A>
<SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">Γυρῶν</SPAN>. <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">γυροῦν</SPAN> W. Heraclides gives <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">γυρεὸν</SPAN>, Plut. <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">γυρεῦον</SPAN>; but one <SPAN CLASS="small">MS.</SPAN> of Plut. gives <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">γύρων</SPAN> with a marginal gloss '<SPAN LANG="la" CLASS="Latin">sc.</SPAN> <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">πετρῶν</SPAN>,' which suggests that the word is a proper name. <I>Od.</I> 4.500 mentions the <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">Γύραι</SPAN> (<I>i.e.</I> the 'round-backed rocks')


<A ID="p425x"></A>where Aias Oileus perished. The word is missing in the <SPAN CLASS="small">MSS.</SPAN> of T.


<P CLASS="ivy">❦</P>


	<P CLASS="justify">
	<A CLASS="note" ID="note98" HREF="
E/Roman/Texts/Theophrastus/De_signis*.html#ref98
"onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,BackRef,WIDTH,175)"
onMouseOut="nd();">98</A>
<SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">ὁμοῖον</SPAN> has perhaps dropped out after <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">ὁμόχρων ᾖ</SPAN>; the adjective seems to agree with <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">νέφος</SPAN>.


<P CLASS="ivy">❦</P>


