E/Roman/Texts/Theophrastus/De_signis*.html#ref103
"onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,BackRef,WIDTH,175)"
onMouseOut="nd();">103</A>
<SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">σπάλακα</SPAN> Vulg.; <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">σπάκα</SPAN> Bas. Ald.; <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">σκολόπακα</SPAN> (woodcock?) conj. Furl.


<P CLASS="ivy">❦</P>


	<P CLASS="justify">
	<A CLASS="note" ID="note104" HREF="
E/Roman/Texts/Theophrastus/De_signis*.html#ref104
"onMouseOver="return Ebox(INARRAY,BackRef,WIDTH,175)"
onMouseOut="nd();">104</A>
<I>i.e.</I> (reading <SPAN LANG="el" CLASS="Greek">σκολόπακα</SPAN>) for the bird to find worms in with its long beak (Sch.). It is hard to say, without illustration,


<A ID="p427x"></A>which is the more convincing of the creatures suggested.


